    <Program class="ca.nrcan.gc.OEE.ERS.ErsProgram">
        <Labels>
            <English>EnerGuide Rating System</English>
            <French>Système de cote ÉnerGuide</French>
        </Labels>
        <Version major="15" minor="4" build="5">
            <Labels>
                <English>v15.4b5</English>
                <French>v15.4b5</French>
            </Labels>
        </Version>
        <SdkVersion major="1" minor="16">
            <Labels>
                <English>v1.16</English>
                <French>v1.16</French>
            </Labels>
        </SdkVersion>
        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="1">
                    <English>Unknown</English>
                    <French>Inconnu</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house fairly air tight, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed around the windows of the home. Older weather stripping appears to be the main culprit of air leakage around the windows, however the frame itself on some (living room, dining room, master walk in closet and master window on right side of home). A couple of the windows had air leakaged observed around the insert (between the window frame and wood), these windows were the front window (by the piano), as well as the dining room window. 

One additional spot leakage was observed is around the hardware for the casement windows in the basement. 

For sealing the window frames, hardware and inserts, I suggest using standard caulking to help create a seal. 



Another area some air leakage was observed is from the fireplace inserts. The flue dampers appeared to do a decent job blocking outside air during our test, however ensure these are closed when not in operation. 

The fireplace in the back living room appears to have a deticated hole for combustion air to enter to ctreate an efficient burn, however is not a sealed connection to the fireplace. This creates uncontrolled air leakage into the home, I suggest investigating if a deticate combustion air pipe can be connected from this hole, directly to the fireplace insert. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 270&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;9.1 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;









&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be double construction, with 2x8 and 2x4 studs with R28 of fiberglass batt insulation, as well as 2x2 strapping with no insulation (the front window and back patio door bump out appear to be 2x8 construction). I have modelled adding 1.5 inches (R7.5) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R7.5 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 220&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;7.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,125 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;







&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</mainWalls>
                <windows>

As all of your windows are older triple pane ones that do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of them with ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing all 20 windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 690&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;23.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Current Oil Boiler and Domestic Hot Water&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



You currently have a 182 litre electric hot water tank, which is also indirectly heated by the Oil Boiler. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly.

It would be ideal to use your boiler to heat your hot water year round, instead of turning off the boiler during the summer months and using the electricity to heat your hot water (winter is assumed to be 9 months of the year).



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By using your current boiler to heat your hot water year round, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 250&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;95% Propane Boiler and Domestic Hot Water&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



If you choose to upgrade your boiler to a condensing 95% AFUE Propane boiler, and continue to heat your hot water indrectly with the new boiler, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save an additional $ 150&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;4.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. 



























&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Boiler&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

Your current oil fired boiler was originally rated at 83% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler, the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1400&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;42.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Pellet Stove&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

If you decideto install a ULC Certified pellet stove. Ensure that the installation complies with local building and fire codes and includes a dedicated combustion air duct. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By installing a ULC Certified pellet stove and using it for 50% of your heating load, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 690 &lt;/b&gt; per year in energy savings and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fireplace Inserts&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



&lt;b&gt;With the Current Oil Boiler&lt;/b&gt;

By using your fireplaces for 50% of your heating load, you could potentially save $ 560 per year in energy savings.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.12 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord 

Pellets - $5.67 per bag



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Disclaimer&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Your Energy Advisor&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



&lt;b&gt;Michael Goodwin

Energy Management Specialist&lt;/b&gt;

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: michael.goodwin@aea.nt.ca



&lt;font color="lightseagreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
